# Plans

A Plan is Main's durable cross-session memory for one substantive user goal. Native host task state and chat history are temporary; the active Markdown Plan records what must survive compaction, a new session, or a new Main Agent.

## When to use a Plan

Create or resume one active Plan for cross-cutting, risky, long-running, multi-agent, public-contract, persistent-data, security, infrastructure, deployment, or otherwise substantive work. Small local reversible changes may proceed without one.

Do not create a second task database, packet directory, lease, journal, scheduler, or orchestration layer.

## Ownership and lifecycle

- Main alone serializes Plan edits and owns goal, scope, non-goals, acceptance, decomposition, dispatch, integration, decisions, checkpoint, exact next action, verification judgment, and terminal transition.
- Sub Agents receive one bounded Task Packet and return Plan-ready evidence; they do not concurrently edit the Plan.
- One substantive goal maps to one file in `exec-plans/active/`.
- Terminal Plans move to `exec-plans/completed/` with actual status `complete`, `cancelled`, or `superseded`.
- Historical Plans remain readable. Supersede or annotate history; do not rewrite old results to match a new process.

## First-Plan handoff

Main—not package runtime—creates and resumes Plans. Before dispatching a substantive goal, Main searches both `exec-plans/active/` and `exec-plans/completed/` for ordinary Markdown Plans matching the goal. Resume exactly one matching active Plan; stop if multiple active Plans match. Completed Plans are historical and are searched to prevent ID reuse, not resumed.

Otherwise choose the lowest unused current-year numeric ID across both directories, copy `_template.md` (`exec-plans/_template.md`) to a new file in `exec-plans/active/`, and never overwrite/reuse an ID. Keep state in visible Markdown. Never use a Plan CLI/hidden state/automatic dispatcher/closure.

## Dynamic procedure Skills

Only complete Confirmed structured procedure records can produce a project-owned instruction-only `.claude/skills/<slug>/SKILL.md` through resumed `reporivet setup`. Do not pre-generate a project-specific Skill. Keep its frontmatter instruction-only and least privilege; no procedure is inferred or executed, and existing differing, stale, or arbitrary Skills are preserved rather than deleted.

## Broad-milestone native-Agent dispatch

For every milestone classified as broad, this is a common installed-project rule:

`T<n> (broad milestone) -> T<n>-A/B/C/... (owned child packets, all ready leaves dispatched concurrently) -> T<n>-I (integration) -> T<n>-V1/V2/... (parallel fresh verification)`

Every child row retains an explicit owner and matching bounded packet. Main owns the overall task tree, Main alone serializes Plan edits, and Main dispatches the complete dependency-ready leaf set concurrently using only the host's native Agent execution. This rule does not apply to inherently single or serial milestones.

If a child is itself broad, only a packet explicitly marked `Role: Task Owner` and `May delegate: yes` may run its predeclared bounded descendant packets such as `T<n>-A-1`; ordinary leaf Agents do not delegate. Descendants inherit parent scope, protected paths, and acceptance and cannot broaden them.

Parallel mutable siblings require disjoint allowed-write sets, frozen shared interfaces, and separate worktrees. Read-only review and verification lanes may run concurrently. Siblings converge on an explicit integration node; fresh verification nodes depend on the integrated candidate.

Reporivet installs no scheduler, task store, lease, lock, or automatic dispatcher; it also installs no dispatcher, task DB, runner, generated CI, Gate, evidence archive, deployment engine, or automatic closure.

## Minimum active Plan content

Use `exec-plans/_template.md`. Keep it concise but restartable:

- original goal and observable acceptance criteria;
- scope and non-goals;
- task rows retaining Owner, State, Depends on, Outcome, Result, and an optional Parallel group;
- decisions and discoveries;
- current checkpoint and exact next action;
- integration and verification summaries, candidate identity, and residual risks.

A Task Packet names outcome, non-goals, exact reads, allowed writes, protected paths, acceptance criteria, verification commands, stop conditions, and compact return evidence. Broad-milestone child packets also name Role, Parent, Parallel group, May delegate, inherited boundaries/write isolation, and Return; an inherently single or serial milestone may omit hierarchy-only fields.

## Completion

Completion requires observable acceptance, integrated project-owned verification, independent review when required, current-state documentation parity, recorded follow-ups, and Main or human evidence judgment. No repository-local completion engine, model judge, automatic closure command, or evidence archive decides completion.
